        <!-- 1. OVERVIEW SECTION -->
        <section id="overview" class="docs-section active">
            <div class="docs-header">
                <h2>Overview & Core Concepts</h2>
                <p>Learn the fundamental architectural layers unifiying the Zero Data Fabric orchestration engine.</p>
            </div>
            
            <p>
                The Zero Data Fabric acts as a decentralized, virtualized query mapping and storage orchestration layer. 
                It unifies heterogeneous storage backends (including Postgres, MySQL, OracleDB, Snowflake, MongoDB, and Elasticsearch) 
                into a single, logical relational namespace per tenant.
            </p>

            <div class="docs-alert">
                <div class="docs-alert-title">💡 Core Architecture Goal</div>
                Zero Data Fabric allows teams to declare their schema state in simple JSON manifests, automatically provisions resources across physical endpoints, handles cross-engine queries dynamically, and propagates state mutations transactionally via SAGA consistency workflows.
            </div>

            <h3>Architectural Layers</h3>
            <p>The orchestrator operates on three primary engine abstractions:</p>
            <table class="docs-table">
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th style="width:25%;">Component</th>
                        <th style="width:35%;">Role</th>
                        <th style="width:40%;">Details</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>Metadata Engine</strong></td>
                        <td>Introspects & Provisioning</td>
                        <td>Parses manifests, diffs schemas, and generates transactional migration steps. Governs table, sequence, triggers, functions, views, and index targets.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>Query Engine</strong></td>
                        <td>AST Parser & Executor</td>
                        <td>Translates relational Abstract Syntax Trees (ASTs) into engine-specific optimized dialects, compiles bind-joins, and aggregates cross-source datasets in-memory.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>Replication Engine</strong></td>
                        <td>CDC & Downstream Sync</td>
                        <td>Subscribes to write-ahead logs (CDC) or transaction streams and synchronizes mutations downstream (e.g. to Elasticsearch and Snowflake) with low latency.</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <h3>logical vs. physical schemas</h3>
            <p>
                In the Data Fabric, developers interact with a unified <strong>Logical Namespace</strong> (e.g. <code>"tenant_tenant_A_global_supply_chain"</code>). The engine resolves the actual physical datasource connections under the hood based on registry endpoints. This decouples service integration from infrastructure changes.
            </p>
        </section>

        <!-- 2. AST QUERIES SECTION -->
        <section id="ast-queries" class="docs-section">
            <div class="docs-header">
                <h2>AST Query Language Schema</h2>
                <p>Developer query protocol for retrieving federated datasets via AST payloads.</p>
            </div>

            <p>
                To perform queries, send a <code>POST</code> request containing a <code>queryConfig</code> payload containing the relational AST directly to the endpoint:
                <code class="docs-badge">POST /api/analytics/query</code>.
            </p>

            <div class="code-container">
                <button class="copy-btn" onclick="copyCode(this)">Copy</button>
                <pre class="code-block"><code>{
  "queryConfig": {
    "type": "SELECT",
    "schema": "Global_Supply_Chain",
    "limit": 50,
    "query": {
      "select": ["*"],
      "from": { "resource": "shipments", "source": "Fabric_Hub_Postgres" }
    }
  }
}</code></pre>
            </div>

            <h3>Top-Level AST Structure</h3>
            <table class="docs-table">
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th style="width:20%;">Parameter</th>
                        <th style="width:20%;">Type</th>
                        <th style="width:20%;">Required</th>
                        <th>Description</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td><code>type</code></td>
                        <td><code>String</code></td>
                        <td>Yes</td>
                        <td>Must be <code>"SELECT"</code> (or mutation types for backend integration).</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><code>schema</code></td>
                        <td><code>String</code></td>
                        <td>Yes</td>
                        <td>The logical schema grouping of target resources.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><code>limit</code></td>
                        <td><code>Integer</code></td>
                        <td>Yes</td>
                        <td>Top-level row cap enforced to guarantee query safety limits.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><code>query</code></td>
                        <td><code>Object</code></td>
                        <td>Yes</td>
                        <td>The relational query details object containing <code>from</code>, <code>select</code>, etc.</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <h3>Core AST Query Properties</h3>
            <p>Configure the following attributes inside the <code>query</code> object block:</p>

            <h4>1. <code>from</code> — Target Resource</h4>
            <p>Describes the primary driver table/collection to fetch from:</p>
            <div class="code-container">
                <pre class="code-block"><code>"from": { 
  "resource": "orders", 
  "source": "Retail_Core", 
  "alias": "o" 
}</code></pre>
            </div>

            <h4>2. <code>select</code> — Projections & Aggregates</h4>
            <p>An array of column names or structured aggregation objects. Omit or pass <code>["*"]</code> to select all columns:</p>
            <div class="code-container">
                <pre class="code-block"><code>"select": [
  "id",
  "region",
  { "aggregate": "SUM", "column": "total_amount", "alias": "revenue" }
]</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="docs-alert">
                <div class="docs-alert-title">ℹ️ Supported Aggregate Functions</div>
                Allowed values: <code>COUNT</code>, <code>SUM</code>, <code>AVG</code>, <code>MIN</code>, <code>MAX</code>, <code>COUNT_DISTINCT</code>. Use <code>"column": "*"</code> for standard row count.
            </div>

            <h4>3. <code>where</code> — Predicate Filters</h4>
            <p>Array of filter conditions matching the structure below. Filters are automatically combined with logical <code>AND</code>:</p>
            <div class="code-container">
                <pre class="code-block"><code>"where": [
  { "column": "status", "operator": "EQ", "value": "SHIPPED" },
  { "column": "total_amount", "operator": "GTE", "value": 1000 }
]</code></pre>
            </div>

            <h4>4. <code>joins</code> — Cross-Source Bind Joins</h4>
            <p>Join configurations. The fabric engine executes cross-source joins as optimized <strong>bind-joins</strong>, querying keys first and passing them in batches to the downstream source:</p>
            <div class="code-container">
                <pre class="code-block"><code>"joins": [
  {
    "type": "INNER",
    "resource": "web_events",
    "source": "Web_Analytics",
    "alias": "w",
    "on": { "left": "o.customer_id", "operator": "EQ", "right": "w.customer_id" }
  }
]</code></pre>
            </div>
            
            <h4>5. Set Operations — Unions & Intersections</h4>
            <p>Run set combinations (<code>union</code>, <code>intersect</code>, <code>except</code>) across entirely separate databases:</p>
            <div class="code-container">
                <pre class="code-block"><code>"intersect": [
  { "select": ["customer_id"], "from": { "resource": "orders", "source": "Retail_Postgres" } },
  { "select": ["customer_id"], "from": { "resource": "web_events", "source": "Web_Mongo" } }
]</code></pre>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- 3. METADATA MANIFESTS SECTION -->
        <section id="metadata-manifests" class="docs-section">
            <div class="docs-header">
                <h2>Metadata Manifest Schema</h2>
                <p>Declarative schema layout for resources, security policies, relationships, and downstream systems.</p>
            </div>

            <p>
                Upload metadata configurations via <code>POST /api/metadata/apply</code> (multipart file upload). 
                The system parses the document, diffs target state with current catalog mappings, and performs the updates.
            </p>

            <div class="code-container">
                <button class="copy-btn" onclick="copyCode(this)">Copy</button>
                <pre class="code-block"><code>{
  "version": "4.0",
  "namespace": "Global_Supply_Chain",
  "targetSource": "Fabric_Hub_Postgres",
  "extensions": ["uuid-ossp"],
  "schemas": [
    {
      "name": "Global_Supply_Chain",
      "targetSource": "Fabric_Hub_Postgres",
      "resources": [
        {
          "type": "TABLE",
          "name": "shipments",
          "columns": [
            { "name": "id", "type": "UUID", "strategy": "UUID_V7", "primaryKey": true },
            { "name": "shipment_no", "type": "BIGINT", "strategy": "IDENTITY_ALWAYS" },
            { "name": "status", "type": "ENUM", "ref": "shipment_status", "default": "DRAFT" },
            { "name": "deleted_at", "type": "TIMESTAMP", "nullable": true, "strategy": "SOFT_DELETE" }
          ],
          "security": {
            "enable_rls": true,
            "policies": [
              { "name": "hide_deleted", "using": "deleted_at IS NULL" }
            ],
            "masking": [
              { "column": "shipment_no", "roles": ["viewer"], "expression": "'REDACTED'" }
            ],
            "grants": [
              { "role": "viewer", "privileges": ["SELECT"] }
            ]
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}</code></pre>
            </div>

            <h3>Resource Schema Definitions</h3>
            
            <h4>TABLE Option Structure</h4>
            <p>Tables support detailed definitions for column datatypes, key generators, constraints, and triggers:</p>
            <table class="docs-table">
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th style="width:25%;">Property</th>
                        <th style="width:25%;">Type</th>
                        <th style="width:50%;">Description</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td><code>name</code></td>
                        <td><code>String</code></td>
                        <td>Table name (snake_case format).</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><code>columns</code></td>
                        <td><code>Array</code></td>
                        <td>List of column definition maps containing types, nullability, strategy, etc.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><code>constraints</code></td>
                        <td><code>Array</code></td>
                        <td>Define <code>CHECK</code> or <code>EXCLUDE</code> rules directly on table writes.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><code>security</code></td>
                        <td><code>Object</code></td>
                        <td>Configuration block for Row Level Security (RLS), column masking, and role grants.</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <h4>ENUM Definition</h4>
            <p>Declare custom state domains dynamically:</p>
            <div class="code-container">
                <pre class="code-block"><code>{ 
  "type": "ENUM", 
  "name": "shipment_status", 
  "values": ["DRAFT", "ASSIGNED", "IN_TRANSIT", "DELIVERED", "CANCELLED"] 
}</code></pre>
            </div>

            <h4>FUNCTION & PROCEDURE Definition</h4>
            <p>Custom store functions/procedures mapped into the database backend:</p>
            <div class="code-container">
                <pre class="code-block"><code>{
  "type": "FUNCTION",
  "name": "calc_tax",
  "arguments": [{ "name": "p_amount", "type": "NUMERIC" }],
  "returnType": "NUMERIC",
  "body": "BEGIN RETURN p_amount * 0.15; END;"
}</code></pre>
            </div>

            <h4>VIEW & MATERIALIZED_VIEW</h4>
            <p>Relational virtualizations, compiled from standard AST queries:</p>
            <div class="code-container">
                <pre class="code-block"><code>{
  "type": "VIEW",
  "name": "active_shipments",
  "query": {
    "select": ["id", "shipment_no"],
    "from": { "resource": "shipments" },
    "where": [{ "column": "status", "operator": "NE", "value": "CANCELLED" }]
  }
}</code></pre>
            </div>

            <h4>Recursive View Schema</h4>
            <p>Define hierarchies using union base and recursive leg structures:</p>
            <div class="code-container">
                <pre class="code-block"><code>{
  "type": "VIEW",
  "name": "org_tree",
  "recursive": true,
  "query": {
    "with": [
      {
        "name": "emp_path",
        "columns": ["id", "name", "manager_id", "level"],
        "base": {
          "select": ["id", "name", "manager_id", { "expression": "1", "alias": "level" }],
          "from": { "resource": "employees" },
          "where": [{ "column": "manager_id", "operator": "IS_NULL" }]
        },
        "unionAll": {
          "select": ["e.id", "e.name", "e.manager_id", { "expression": "ep.level+1" }],
          "from": { "resource": "employees", "alias": "e" },
          "joins": [
            {
              "type": "INNER",
              "resource": "emp_path",
              "alias": "ep",
              "on": { "left": "e.manager_id", "operator": "EQ", "right": "ep.id" }
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ],
    "select": ["*"],
    "from": { "resource": "emp_path" }
  }
}</code></pre>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- 4. SYNC & REPLICATION SECTION -->
        <section id="sync-replication" class="docs-section">
            <div class="docs-header">
                <h2>Sync & Replication Engine</h2>
                <p>Streaming data pipelines, CDC capture, and downstreams synchronization.</p>
            </div>

            <p>
                Zero Data Fabric includes a high-performance **Replication Engine** which captures modifications on your core database (Hub) and automatically propagates them downstream to analytical nodes.
            </p>

            <div class="docs-alert">
                <div class="docs-alert-title">🔗 Downstream Targets Supported</div>
                Currently unifies replication to <strong>Elasticsearch</strong> (for full-text search) and <strong>Snowflake</strong> (for analytical workloads).
            </div>

            <h3>Downstream Declaration in Manifest</h3>
            <p>Register analytical legs under the <code>downstream</code> array block in the manifest:</p>
            <div class="code-container">
                <button class="copy-btn" onclick="copyCode(this)">Copy</button>
                <pre class="code-block"><code>"downstream": [
  {
    "type": "ELASTICSEARCH",
    "enabled": true,
    "fallback": "PRIMARY_SQL"
  },
  {
    "type": "SNOWFLAKE",
    "enabled": true,
    "strategy": "CDC"
  }
]</code></pre>
            </div>

            <h3>Replication Architecture Mappings</h3>
            <p>The engine offers two latency modes:</p>
            <table class="docs-table">
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th style="width:30%;">Mode</th>
                        <th style="width:35%;">Pipeline Method</th>
                        <th style="width:35%;">Latency Target</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>CDC (Change Data Capture)</strong></td>
                        <td>Kafka broker listening to write-ahead logs</td>
                        <td>&lt; 500ms (Sub-second streaming)</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>Scheduled Batch Sync</strong></td>
                        <td>Polled delta sync via soft-delete metadata columns</td>
                        <td>Configurable (e.g. every 5 minutes / 1 hour)</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </section>

        <!-- 5. COOKBOOK / EXAMPLES SECTION -->
        <section id="cookbook" class="docs-section">
            <div class="docs-header">
                <h2>Query & Integration Cookbook</h2>
                <p>Walkthrough guides and copy-pasteable snippets for all primary runtime scenarios.</p>
            </div>

            <h3>1. Registering Sources</h3>
            <p>Provision connections to external database nodes. Targets are securely registered as metadata objects:</p>
            <div class="code-container">
                <button class="copy-btn" onclick="copyCode(this)">Copy</button>
                <pre class="code-block"><code>// POST /api/connections
{
  "name": "Warehouse_DB",
  "type": "mysql",
  "config": {
    "host": "mysql-prod-endpoint",
    "port": 3306,
    "username": "fabric_reader",
    "password": "secure_password",
    "database": "warehouse"
  }
}</code></pre>
            </div>

            <h3>2. Multi-Datasource Federated Query</h3>
            <p>Join a PostgreSQL user table with a MongoDB audit collection. The Query Planner automatically optimizes resource scanning:</p>
            <div class="code-container">
                <button class="copy-btn" onclick="copyCode(this)">Copy</button>
                <pre class="code-block"><code>// POST /api/analytics/query
{
  "queryConfig": {
    "type": "SELECT",
    "schema": "public",
    "limit": 10,
    "query": {
      "select": ["u.id", "u.name", "a.event_name", "a.timestamp"],
      "from": { "resource": "users", "source": "Fabric_Hub_Postgres", "alias": "u" },
      "joins": [
        {
          "type": "INNER",
          "resource": "audit_logs",
          "source": "Audit_Mongo",
          "alias": "a",
          "on": { "left": "u.id", "operator": "EQ", "right": "a.user_id" }
        }
      ],
      "where": [
        { "column": "u.status", "operator": "EQ", "value": "ACTIVE" }
      ]
    }
  }
}</code></pre>
            </div>

            <h3>3. Federated Aggregations</h3>
            <p>Retrieve total aggregates partitioned across separate databases, triggering parallel computation on the engines:</p>
            <div class="code-container">
                <button class="copy-btn" onclick="copyCode(this)">Copy</button>
                <pre class="code-block"><code>// POST /api/analytics/query
{
  "queryConfig": {
    "type": "SELECT",
    "schema": "public",
    "query": {
      "union": [
        {
          "from": { "resource": "sales_us", "source": "US_Postgres" },
          "groupBy": ["category"],
          "select": ["category", { "aggregate": "SUM", "column": "amount", "alias": "total" }]
        },
        {
          "from": { "resource": "sales_eu", "source": "EU_MySQL" },
          "groupBy": ["category"],
          "select": ["category", { "aggregate": "SUM", "column": "amount", "alias": "total" }]
        }
      ]
    }
  }
}</code></pre>
            </div>

            <h3>4. Event Triggers</h3>
            <p>Configure actions to fire automatically when mutations happen inside specific tables:</p>
